TapAuth and the Debian PAM stack
================================

TapAuth ships a PAM module (`pam_tapauth.so`) that lets a paired Android
phone approve an authentication request. To make it reachable from the
places where it matters, the `tapauth` package patches three PAM service
files:

    /etc/pam.d/sudo       (conffile owned by the "sudo" package)
    /etc/pam.d/su         (conffile owned by the "util-linux" package)
    /etc/pam.d/polkit-1   (conffile owned by policykit-1/polkitd; on some
                           releases the vendor stack is shipped as
                           /usr/lib/pam.d/polkit-1 instead, and tapauth
                           seeds an /etc/pam.d/polkit-1 override from it)

Into each file it inserts one line:

    auth    sufficient    pam_tapauth.so

`sufficient` means a successful TapAuth approval authenticates the request,
while every other method (password, etc.) keeps working exactly as before if
the phone is unavailable. The package deliberately does not touch any other
stack: `login` is never patched, and fingerprint stacks (kde-fingerprint,
gdm-fingerprint, fingerprint-auth, ...) stay stock. Lock screens and greeters
are served by the opt-in virtual fprintd bridge described below, not by
patching their PAM files.


Why not pam-auth-update?
------------------------

Debian's `pam-auth-update` mechanism manages the shared `common-*` stacks
(`common-auth`, `common-account`, ...), not the service files
`/etc/pam.d/{sudo,su,polkit-1}`. Registering a `pam-auth-update` profile
therefore cannot express TapAuth's intended scope: in earlier releases it
also ended up wiring the module into `login`. No profile is shipped any
more and the three-file scope above is applied directly, which
is deliberate. Upgrading from a <= 0.10.x release that shipped a
`/usr/share/pam-configs/tapauth` profile automatically regenerates the
pam-auth-update-managed stacks so the stale generated line in
`/etc/pam.d/common-auth` is removed (on upgrade and on plain `apt remove`).


One-time backups (.tapauth-bak)
-------------------------------

The first time TapAuth patches one of the three files it takes a one-time
snapshot next to it:

    /etc/pam.d/<service>.tapauth-bak

The snapshot is created once and never refreshed on ordinary upgrades, so it
represents the pre-TapAuth state. It is *not* used to restore the file on
removal (see below).


Removal: strip, don't restore
-----------------------------

On removal the maintainer scripts remove only the TapAuth-inserted
`pam_tapauth.so` line(s) from the three files and delete the
`.tapauth-bak` snapshots. They deliberately do **not** copy the snapshot
back over the file: because the snapshot was taken once at install time,
restoring it would silently revert every administrator edit and every
vendor/distribution update made since. Removing only the inserted line
cannot leave a dangling reference to a removed module, so it cannot lock
anyone out.

If the `/etc/pam.d/polkit-1` file was itself seeded by TapAuth from
`/usr/lib/pam.d/polkit-1`, then after stripping the line the override is
byte-identical to the vendor stack and is deleted (guarded by `cmp -s`), so
the vendor file applies again.

The standalone `install.sh`/`uninstall.sh` tooling offers an explicit,
opt-in rollback (`uninstall.sh --restore-pam-backups`) for users who really
want the install-time snapshot copied back. The distribution packages do not
do this automatically.


Vendor-drift re-apply
---------------------

Because the three files are conffiles, a distribution upgrade of the owning
package can legitimately replace a vendor file (losing our line) or ship a
new `/usr/lib/pam.d/polkit-1` (which our override would otherwise shadow
forever). To keep TapAuth wired across such upgrades, the package registers
dpkg triggers on `/etc/pam.d/{sudo,su,polkit-1}` and
`/usr/lib/pam.d/{sudo,su,polkit-1}`. When one fires, the `postinst` ("triggered"
state) re-seeds the override from the new vendor file when one exists and
re-applies the TapAuth line, but **only** when the `.tapauth-bak` snapshot
shows TapAuth patched that file before, and only when the line is missing
(idempotent). For `su` the line is re-inserted after `pam_rootok.so` /
`pam_wheel.so` and before the first auth include, matching the install-time
rule, so a phone grant for `root` can never bypass those checks. The Fedora
and Arch packages carry equivalent `%triggerin` / libalpm hook machinery.


Opt-in virtual fprintd bridge
-----------------------------

The base package does **not** claim the `net.reactivated.Fprint` D-Bus name
and ships no D-Bus activation file, so a real local `fprintd` (and a
physical fingerprint reader) is never shadowed. The daemon's
`enable_fprintd_bridge` setting is tri-state:

  * unset (default) - the bridge is enabled only if the marker file
    /usr/share/tapauth/fprintd-emulation.enabled exists;
  * true  - force the bridge on;
  * false - force it off.

The optional `tapauth-fprintd-emulation` package ships that marker (and a
second build of the module installed as `pam_fprintd.so`), which turns the
bridge on and lets stock fingerprint PAM stacks be served by TapAuth. The
state is read at daemon startup, so a change takes effect on the next
`tapauthd` restart. The base package declares no `Conflicts` against fprintd
or libpam-fprintd; only the optional emulation package does.


Socket access is a manual, per-user opt-in
------------------------------------------

The daemon socket `/run/tapauthd/tapauthd.sock` is owned
`root:tapauthd-clients` with mode 0660. Membership in `tapauthd-clients` is
**not** granted automatically - neither the packages nor `install.sh` add
every interactive user. Each desktop user who needs the configuration GUI or
user-session lock-screen unlock (for example KDE's `kscreenlocker_worker`,
which runs as the logged-in user) must opt in and re-login:

    sudo usermod -aG tapauthd-clients $USER

The source `install.sh` adds only the user who ran it. Root-run greeters and
auth helpers (GDM/SDDM/LightDM) are unaffected. Membership is not removed on
uninstall or purge (only the group itself is removed on purge).


Relationship to Debian Policy
-----------------------------

Debian Policy §10.7.3 ("Behavior") requires that local changes to
configuration files be preserved during upgrades and that conffiles be
preserved on removal (deleted only on purge). It also states that maintainer
scripts must not alter a conffile of *any* package, including their own.
§10.7.4 ("Sharing configuration files") explains that when several packages
need to modify the same file, one package should own it and provide a
modification helper, and that packages directly modifying another package's
conffile should otherwise conflict.

TapAuth knowingly deviates from the strict form of that rule: its whole
purpose is to wire a PAM module into conffiles owned by `sudo`,
`util-linux` and `policykit-1`, and there is no shared helper available for
those service files (pam-auth-update only covers `common-*`; see above).
The deviation is kept as small and as reversible as policy allows:

  * edits are limited to a single inserted line per file;
  * a one-time `.tapauth-bak` snapshot is kept for manual rollback;
  * removal strips only the inserted line and never clobbers the file with
    the snapshot, so administrator and vendor changes survive;
  * the vendor-drift triggers re-apply only when TapAuth patched the file
    before, and only when the line is missing.

Administrators who prefer not to have TapAuth edit those files can remove
the inserted lines manually after installation (the module keeps working for
any stack that calls it) or simply not install the package.


Manual recovery
---------------

If you are locked out, boot a rescue shell and restore the pre-TapAuth
state. Prefer stripping TapAuth's own line:

    for f in /etc/pam.d/sudo /etc/pam.d/su /etc/pam.d/polkit-1; do
        [ -f "$f" ] && sed -i '/pam_tapauth\.so/d' "$f"
    done

Or, if a `.tapauth-bak` snapshot still exists and you explicitly want the
install-time state back:

    for f in /etc/pam.d/sudo /etc/pam.d/su /etc/pam.d/polkit-1; do
        [ -s "$f.tapauth-bak" ] && cp -p "$f.tapauth-bak" "$f"
    done

